---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Postern — 8-slide overview"
  description="Seven slides summarising the Postern research artifact: problem, architecture, Lean proofs, Rust mirror, capability-bounded data flow, open problems."
  bare={true}
>
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@5.1.0/dist/reveal.css"
  />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/reveal.js@5.1.0/dist/theme/white.css"
    id="reveal-theme"
  />

  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="slides">

      <!-- S1: Title / thesis -->
      <section class="slide-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Research artifact &middot; 2026</p>
        <h1>Postern</h1>
        <h2>Lean-verified Biscuit-Datalog policies for agentic data lakehouses</h2>
        <p class="claim">
          <strong>First Lean-mechanised soundness theorem</strong> for a
          plan-level access-control rewriter in an LLM-agent-facing data
          lakehouse.
        </p>
        <p class="claim small">
          Two layers behind one gateway: a <em>rewriter</em> (inspired by
          Cedar) bounds <em>what data</em> reaches the agent, and a
          <em>capability-bounded data flow</em> layer (inspired by Odersky
          et&nbsp;al.) bounds <em>what the agent's code may do</em> with
          released values. Biscuit attenuation, audience, expiry, key
          rotation, cross-relation joins, and DP aggregation are explicit
          open problems (&sect;6).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S2: Problem -->
      <section>
        <h2>Agents need a governance layer at the lake</h2>
        <p class="small">
          Agent rights are <em>context-driven</em> — principal, task,
          scope — and the same agent code may legitimately serve
          different views on different calls. Static
          <em>identity&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;role&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;permission</em>
          can't encode that; per-engine RLS doesn't survive ETL;
          tenant segregation forfeits cross-source joins. Policy has
          to move to the <em>query plane</em>.
        </p>
        <pre class="mermaid" is:raw>
flowchart LR
  classDef ok fill:#eef,stroke:#446,color:#333
  classDef agent fill:#fec,stroke:#b15c2e,color:#333
  classDef bad fill:#fee,stroke:#a33,color:#333,stroke-dasharray:4

  subgraph rbac["RBAC (stable, fits humans)"]
    direction LR
    u[user]:::ok --> rl[role]:::ok --> pm[perms]:::ok
  end

  subgraph reality["Agent (context-driven, per call)"]
    direction LR
    pr[principal]:::agent --> ag[agent]:::agent
    tk[task] -.-> ag
    sc[scope] -.-> ag
    ag --> vv{{"view varies per call"}}:::bad
  end
        </pre>
      </section>

      <!-- S3: Architecture (adapted from paper §Design + §4) -->
      <section>
        <h2>Architecture</h2>
        <p class="muted small">
          Adapted from paper &sect;Design (Layer&nbsp;1 rewriter) and
          &sect;4 (Layer&nbsp;2 capability-bounded data flow).
        </p>
        <pre class="mermaid" is:raw>
flowchart LR
  agent[LLM agent] -->|plan + biscuit| gw{{Postern gateway}}
  subgraph TCB[trusted base]
    gw -->|sig verify| bc[biscuit-auth]
    bc -->|principal + token facts| ev["biscuit Datalog eval<br/>(planned)"]
    pol[(policy: right facts)] --> ev
    ev -->|allow-set| rw["plan rewriter<br/>(Lean ref &harr; Rust mirror)"]
    cat[(catalog)] --> rw
    rw -->|Option Plan| exe[DuckDB / Polars]
    exe --> guard["capability-bounded sink<br/>Cap·Tagged · opaque receipt"]
  end
  guard -->|receipt| agent
        </pre>
        <p class="small">
          <strong>Layer&nbsp;1</strong> (rewriter) constrains <em>what
          data</em> reaches the agent; <strong>Layer&nbsp;2</strong>
          (capability-bounded sink) constrains <em>what the agent's
          code may do</em> with released values. Rust mirror is
          hand-written and pinned to the Lean reference by the
          <code>postern-diff</code> conformance harness (18/18 cases);
          property-based differential testing is an open problem
          (&sect;6).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S4: Sample policy (Datalog at biscuit) -->
      <section>
        <h2>Sample policy — financial institution</h2>
        <p class="small muted">
          Surface syntax (left) is sugar over ground
          <code>right(principal, relation, column)</code> Datalog
          facts (right) — the in-scope fragment of biscuit-auth's
          Datalog dialect. Attenuation, expiry, audience, and key
          rotation are <a href="#open-problems">open problems</a>
          (paper &sect;6).
        </p>
        <div class="policy-grid">
          <div>
            <h3 class="policy-title">policy.postern</h3>
            <pre class="policy-snip"><code>{`grant CRM       on users_data
  { id, name, region, age }

grant CardOps   on cards_data
  { card_id, card_type, limit, activated }

grant FraudRisk on transactions_data
  { txn_id, card_id, amount, merchant, timestamp }

grant FraudRisk on users_data
  { id, region }`}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="policy-title">Datalog (biscuit-auth)</h3>
            <pre class="policy-snip"><code>{`right("CRM", "users_data", "id");
right("CRM", "users_data", "name");
right("CRM", "users_data", "region");
right("CRM", "users_data", "age");

right("CardOps", "cards_data", "card_id");
right("CardOps", "cards_data", "card_type");
…
right("FraudRisk", "users_data", "id");
right("FraudRisk", "users_data", "region");`}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted">
          Multiple grants on the same <code>(p, r)</code>
          flat-union — the policy language is monotone grant-only,
          so review is additive: a new grant can only widen.
          Anything outside the union is denied (fail-closed).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S5: What Lean proves -->
      <section>
        <h2>What Lean proves</h2>
        <table class="theorems">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>theorem</th>
              <th>file</th>
              <th>status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>rewrite_sound</code></td>
              <td><code>Postern.lean</code></td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">proved</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>rewrite_filter_sound</code></td>
              <td><code>Postern.lean</code></td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">proved</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>eval_monotone</code></td>
              <td><code>Datalog.lean</code></td>
              <td><span class="badge partial">proof reduces to one sorry</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>eval_sound</code></td>
              <td><code>Datalog.lean</code></td>
              <td><span class="badge todo">stated, sorry</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>eval_terminates</code></td>
              <td><code>Datalog.lean</code></td>
              <td><span class="badge todo">stated, sorry</span></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="small">
          Axioms of proven theorems:
          <code>{`{ propext, Quot.sound }`}</code>.
          <code>eval_monotone</code>'s body is written but reduces
          to <code>herbrandBound_mono</code> — a labelled
          <code>sorry</code> we expose rather than hide. Eight
          supporting iterator lemmas are proven.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S6: Learning from Cedar -->
      <section>
        <h2>Learning from Cedar</h2>
        <p>
          <code>postern-core</code> mirrors the Lean rewriter one-for-one.
          The gateway evaluates policies through
          <code>biscuit_auth::datalog::World</code> behind a Cargo
          feature flag.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>postern-core</code> — Plan IR, policy, <code>rewrite()</code>.</li>
          <li><code>postern-guardrail</code> — paper &sect;4's <em>capability-bounded data flow</em>: branded <code>Cap&lt;'sc, C&gt;</code>, invariant <code>'sc</code>, opaque-receipt sinks (three <code>compile_fail</code> doctests pin the lexical escape attempts).</li>
          <li><code>postern-diff</code> — reference-conformance harness, asserts Rust output is byte-equal to the Lean reference on a hand-curated JSON corpus (18/18 cases: 15 accept, 3 refuse).</li>
          <li><code>postern-wasm</code> — surface this site loads.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">
          The WASM module powering the <a href="/demo">demo</a> is today the
          column-grant DSL evaluator; Biscuit-Datalog migration in progress.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S7: Capability-bounded data flow (paper §4) -->
      <section>
        <h2>Capability-bounded data flow</h2>
        <p class="small">
          The rewriter constrains <em>what data</em> reaches the agent.
          A separate layer constrains <em>what the agent's code may do</em>
          with the values released. Inspired by Odersky et al.'s Scala&nbsp;3
          capture-checking; Rust has none, so we mechanise a weaker analog
          out of three pure-Rust constructions.
        </p>
        <pre class="rust-snip"><code>{`pub fn to_llm<'sc, T, C, S>(
    cap: Cap<'sc, C>,
    data: Tagged<'sc, T, C>,
    serialize: S,
) -> LlmAck
where S: FnOnce(T) -> String;   // T never escapes the sink`}</code></pre>
        <table class="theorems">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>bypass attempt</th>
              <th>mechanism</th>
              <th>pinned by</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>forge a <code>Cap</code></td>
              <td>sealed private constructor</td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">compile_fail</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>read <code>Tagged::value</code></td>
              <td>private <code>value</code> field, sinks only</td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">compile_fail</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>escape brand <code>'sc</code></td>
              <td>invariant <code>PhantomData</code> + <code>for&lt;'sc&gt;</code> scope combinator</td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">compile_fail</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>side-channel via <code>T</code></td>
              <td>opaque receipts (only serialised length escapes)</td>
              <td><span class="badge ok">type signature</span></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="small muted">
          Construction is the one used by <code>ghost-cell</code> for branded
          references. Crate is <code>no_std</code>-compatible (paper &sect;4).
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- S8: Open problems -->
      <section id="open-problems">
        <h2>Open problems</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Block <strong>attenuation</strong> — biscuit blocks can shrink rights mid-flight; the rewriter doesn't yet model this.</li>
          <li><strong>Audience checks</strong> + <strong>expiry</strong> — bind tokens to gateway identity and a time window.</li>
          <li><strong>Key rotation</strong> for the verifier without invalidating in-flight tokens.</li>
          <li><strong>Cross-relation joins</strong> — the current IR is single-relation; multi-relation grants compose non-trivially.</li>
          <li><strong>Aggregation with a DP boundary</strong> — summary outputs without leaking row-level columns.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted small">Paper &sect;6 enumerates the full open-problem list.</p>
        <p class="ribbon">
          <a href="/paper">Read the paper</a> &middot;
          <a href="/paper.pdf">PDF</a> &middot;
          <a href="/demo">Try the WASM demo</a> &middot;
          <a href="https://github.com/debuggingfuture/secure-program-synthesis">GitHub</a>
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    import Reveal from "reveal.js";
    import mermaid from "mermaid";

    mermaid.initialize({
      startOnLoad: false,
      theme: "neutral",
      securityLevel: "strict",
      flowchart: { curve: "linear", htmlLabels: true },
      fontFamily: "ui-monospace, JetBrains Mono, Menlo, monospace",
    });

    // Render mermaid blocks on each slide as it becomes active.
    async function renderMermaidInSlide(slide: Element | null) {
      if (!slide) return;
      const pres = slide.querySelectorAll("pre.mermaid:not([data-rendered])");
      for (const pre of Array.from(pres)) {
        const el = pre as HTMLElement;
        el.setAttribute("data-rendered", "1");
        const src = el.textContent ?? "";
        const id = "mmd-" + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2);
        try {
          const { svg } = await mermaid.render(id, src);
          el.innerHTML = svg;
        } catch (e) {
          el.innerHTML = `<pre style="color:#b03a3a">mermaid error: ${String(e)}</pre>`;
        }
      }
    }

    const deck = new Reveal({
      hash: true,
      slideNumber: "c/t",
      controlsTutorial: false,
      transition: "fade",
      width: 1120,
      height: 720,
      margin: 0.04,
    });
    deck.initialize().then(() => {
      renderMermaidInSlide(deck.getCurrentSlide());
      // reveal.js's `on()` lacks types for its custom events in
      // @types/reveal.js, so we cast through the wider Element form.
      (deck as unknown as {
        on: (
          ev: string,
          h: (e: { currentSlide: Element }) => void,
        ) => void;
      }).on("slidechanged", (e) => renderMermaidInSlide(e.currentSlide));
    });
  </script>

  <style is:global>
    /* Override reveal.js defaults to match site voice. */
    .reveal {
      font-family: var(--font-sans, system-ui, sans-serif);
      color: #222;
    }

    .reveal h1,
    .reveal h2,
    .reveal h3 {
      font-family: ui-serif, "Iowan Old Style", Palatino, serif;
      text-transform: none;
      letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      color: #1a1a1a;
    }

    .reveal h1 {
      font-size: 3rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
    }

    .reveal h2 {
      font-size: 1.7rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid #d9d6cf;
      padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }

    .reveal code {
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", Menlo, monospace;
      font-size: 0.88em;
      background: #f2efe7;
      padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
      border-radius: 2px;
    }

    .reveal pre.rust-snip {
      background: #f7f4ec;
      border: 1px solid #d9d6cf;
      padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", Menlo, monospace;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      line-height: 1.35;
      max-width: 95%;
      margin: 0.6rem auto;
      text-align: left;
      white-space: pre;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    .reveal pre.rust-snip code {
      background: transparent;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .reveal .policy-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 0.8rem;
      margin: 0.5rem auto;
      max-width: 95%;
    }

    .reveal .policy-grid > div {
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .reveal .policy-title {
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", Menlo, monospace;
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: #888;
      margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .reveal pre.policy-snip {
      background: #f7f4ec;
      border: 1px solid #d9d6cf;
      padding: 0.55rem 0.75rem;
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", Menlo, monospace;
      font-size: 0.66rem;
      line-height: 1.38;
      margin: 0;
      text-align: left;
      white-space: pre;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    .reveal pre.policy-snip code {
      background: transparent;
      padding: 0;
    }

    .reveal pre.mermaid {
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #d9d6cf;
      padding: 1rem;
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      max-width: 95%;
      margin: 0.6rem auto;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .reveal pre.mermaid svg {
      max-width: 100% !important;
      height: auto !important;
    }

    .reveal .slide-title {
      text-align: left;
    }

    .reveal .eyebrow {
      font-family: ui-monospace, "JetBrains Mono", Menlo, monospace;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      font-size: 0.74rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      color: #b15c2e;
    }

    .reveal .slide-title h2 {
      border: none;
      font-style: italic;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: #555;
      font-size: 1.4rem;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }

    .reveal .claim {
      max-width: 28em;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
      color: #333;
    }

    .reveal .muted {
      color: #888;
    }

    .reveal .small {
      font-size: 0.86rem;
      color: #555;
    }

    .reveal .bad {
      color: #b03a3a;
    }

    .reveal .good {
      color: #2f6b3a;
    }

    .reveal ul {
      font-size: 0.96rem;
    }

    .reveal .theorems {
      width: 100%;
      font-size: 0.86rem;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      margin: 0.6rem 0;
    }

    .reveal .theorems th,
    .reveal .theorems td {
      border-bottom: 1px solid #e3e0d8;
      padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .reveal .badge {
      font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
      border-radius: 2px;
    }

    .reveal .badge.ok {
      background: rgba(47, 107, 58, 0.12);
      color: #2f6b3a;
    }

    .reveal .badge.todo {
      background: #fff7e6;
      color: #b88330;
    }

    .reveal .badge.partial {
      background: rgba(177, 92, 46, 0.14);
      color: #b15c2e;
    }

    .reveal .ribbon {
      margin-top: 1.4rem;
      font-size: 0.94rem;
    }

    .reveal a {
      color: #b15c2e;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
